# Upstash Redis — each Vercel project is linked to its own Upstash instance
# (guandan-online runs separately from sibling guandan-scorer).
# Get from Vercel project Settings → Storage → Upstash Redis integration.
#
# Either naming pair works. The Vercel Marketplace integration auto-provisions
# the KV_REST_API_* pair (legacy Vercel KV-style names); the UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_*
# pair is what the @upstash/redis SDK docs use. When both are set, UPSTASH-
# prefixed wins. Locally, prefer `vercel env pull .env.local` over hand-editing.
UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_URL=
UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_TOKEN=
# KV_REST_API_URL=
# KV_REST_API_TOKEN=

# Admin / cron secret (SEC-3). Constant-time compared against the
# `Authorization: Bearer ...` header on:
#   - GET /api/cron/cleanup-rooms (hourly Vercel cron)
#   - Future admin endpoints (same pattern as sibling scorer)
#
# BLAST RADIUS — TREAT AS PROJECT-KILLING SECRET
# Anyone holding this token can invoke /api/cron/cleanup-rooms at will, which
# enumerates every active room code in the Upstash index and deletes the
# corresponding room state. There is no per-call delete cap and no rate limit
# in front of the cron route. A leaked token = an attacker can drop every
# in-flight game in production within seconds.
#
# Rotation guidance:
#   1. Generate a fresh value: `openssl rand -hex 32`
#   2. Update in Vercel Production env (Settings → Environment Variables)
#   3. Trigger a redeploy so the new value is bound to running functions
#   4. Update CRON_SECRET alongside if you set it separately
#   5. Rotate immediately on any suspected leak (shared screen, paste in chat,
#      committed by mistake, contractor offboarded)
# If unset, the cron rejects all requests (fail-closed).
ADMIN_TOKEN=
